<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herzegovina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herzegovina is the southern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and Herzegovina, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a> valley and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostar">Mostar</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the principality of Hum (Zachlumia), and it takes its name from the title herceg, duke, used by the nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-vukcic-kosaca">Stjepan Vukčić Kosača</a> in the 15th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered it in the 1480s, and an uprising there in 1875 opened the Great Eastern Crisis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herzegovina.jpg" alt="Herzegovina" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lommes · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzegovina_location_map_simple.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2016 about 700 troops were stationed abroad: 100 with the NATO-led ISAF force in Afghanistan, 210 in Kosovo under KFOR and 160 in Bosnia and <b>Herzegovina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The six republics, Bosnia and <b>Herzegovina</b>, Croatia, Macedonia, Montenegro, Serbia and Slovenia, were constitutionally equal; Serbia alone contained two autonomous provinces, Kosovo and Vojvodina, which the 1974 constitution gave voting power equal to the republics', to the lasting anger of Serbian officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Military service was required of non-Muslims in 1856, but exemptions could be bought, angering poorer Christians, and tax grievances helped spark the <b>Herzegovina</b> Uprising of 1875.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Federal constitutions have since been used to end or prevent conflict, as in Bosnia and <b>Herzegovina</b> in 1995, Iraq in 2005 and Nepal in 2015.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Sokollu Mehmed Pasha, born an Orthodox Christian in <b>Herzegovina</b>, was grand vizier from 1565 to 1579.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>With war looming, the regent Prince Paul sought agreement with the Croatian Peasant Party, and the Cvetković-Maček Agreement of 26 August 1939 merged the Sava and Littoral banovinas with parts of Bosnia, <b>Herzegovina</b>, Srem and the Dubrovnik area into one Banovina of Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/state/banovina-of-croatia">Banovina of Croatia</a></li></ul></section>
